By default ...

In modern hardware, it makes sense to set your xterm
scroll to 5000 lines or more. That's only 400Kbytes
at 80 bytes per line (and the average line is
usually more like 20 bytes) and most of it sits in
SWAP unless you use it. I usually close the xterm
long before 5000 lines is full anyway, freeing that
space.

In a compile, I'd probably either up the scroll
limit even further just when instancing my build
window, or, more intelligently, divert the build
output with tee and a named pipe into a less()
invocation, rather than a file. It can be saved from
within less() if it proves to have archival value.

Doing it your way risks dropping trash files around
that will require a cleanup step later.

